[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-11", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 11 — the window pays only where the writer put locality, and the head window is the open", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-11.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 11 - the window pays only where the writer put locality, and the head window is the open\n\n> Seed: root ADR-2609119000 (2026-09-11) -- what Parquet / Arrow / Iceberg /\n> Paimon / RisingWave do with the index, mapped onto CARv2 packs -- with four\n> hypotheses and their PREDICTIONS written before this ran. The loop is\n> Generate -> Reflect -> Rank -> Evolve -> Meta. The judge is `bench/fitness`:\n> reads and bytes, never wall clock, and every arm must return the same 3\n> rows or the harness exits 2.\n\n## Where the reader stood (iteration 03's price model)\n\n    per-object  = 2N              (discover + fetch per block)\n    packed      = 3P + N          (catalog P, open 2P, one exact range per block)\n\nso packing won only while N/P > 3. The N term is the point of this\niteration: on the live Worker one day earlier (root ADR-2609109900 Q3h) a\n4 MiB window read from a 102 MB pack served a 32-block traversal in 3\nrequests, and the only reason was that the pack had been written root-first.\nThat is a claim about WRITE ORDER, and this loop can test it.\n\n## Generate (the predictions, verbatim from the ADR)\n\n  H1a  A window read over a pack in HASH order does not reduce reads (the\n       window carries unrelated frames) and raises bytes.\n  H1b  The same window over a pack written root-first, breadth-first, cuts\n       the per-block term to the number of contiguous runs. At the fitness\n       shape (N=6, one pack) reads 6 -> 2..3.\n  H2   Partition-aligned packs (one prolly internal node -> one pack) drop P.\n  H3   Stats in the catalog let a query skip packs. Point lookup P -> 1.\n  H4   LSM levels; read amplification = L0 runs + 1.\n\nThis iteration measures H1a and H1b. H2-H4 stay seeds.\n\n## The arms\n\n`ayatori.pack` gained `:window`: `read-block` reads `window` bytes forward\nfrom the located frame, bounded by the payload end so the index is never\nparsed as frames, verifies every whole frame it got (`car2/read-frame`),\nand caches them; a later read of a cached CID costs nothing. `:window 0` is\nthe exact reader iteration 01 measured, unchanged. Two orderings of the same\nblocks: the map's hash order (what the arm always used) and breadth-first\nfrom the snapshot root (siblings adjacent). Window 64 KiB.\n\n## Rank / Evolve -- first run (window read, 51-byte header open)\n\n    subj |  exact-read   bytes |  window/hash   bytes |  window/bfs   bytes\n      50            5   14223             5   28419            3   21107\n     100            7   23269             4   85084            3   42896\n     200            7   52073             6  158642            4   94262\n     400            8   80434             5  193408            4  123381\n     800            8  110996             7  328681            5  197609\n\nH1b CONFIRMED in direction: bfs 5 vs exact 8 at 800, and bfs <= hash at\nevery size. H1a PARTLY REFUTED: hash order did cut reads (8 -> 7 at 800,\n7 -> 4 at 100) -- a 64 KiB window over a 110 KB pack covers half the pack,\nso unrelated frames are still frames the query wants often enough. The\nprediction said \"does not reduce\"; it should have said \"reduces by chance,\nand pays for it\": hash bytes are 3.0x exact, bfs 1.8x.\n\nEvolved hypothesis, from the Worker's design: the OPEN is still two\nrequests (51-byte header, then the index). With a window, the first read\ncan be the head window itself -- the header is at its front, the root frame\nright behind it, and a pack that fits arrives whole, index included.\n\n  H1c  First read = head window. Predicted: bfs 5 -> 4 at 800; packs\n       smaller than the window open and read in ONE request.\n\n## Rank / Evolve -- second run (H1c: the head window is the open)\n\n    subj |  exact-read   bytes |  window/hash   bytes |  window/bfs   bytes\n      50            5   14223             1   21166            1   21166\n     100            7   23269             1   42955            1   42955\n     200            7   52073             4   93055            3   94211\n     400            8   80434             5  258893            3  123330\n     800            8  110996             7  394166            4  197558\n\nH1c CONFIRMED exactly: bfs 4 at 800 (head window, index, two windows), and\n1 request for every pack under 64 KiB -- order irrelevant there, because\nthe window is the whole pack. Against per-object's 12 that is 3.0x on\nreads where the exact reader managed 1.5x, at 1.8x the bytes.\n\n## The window sweep (400 and 800 subjects, bfs vs hash vs exact)\n\n    window   exact  |  hash reads  bytes  |  bfs reads  bytes\n    16 KiB     8    |     11      224139  |     8       174987\n    32 KiB     8    |      8      261511  |     6       195975\n    64 KiB     8    |      7      394166  |     4       197558\n   128 KiB     8    |      5      480454  |     3       246965\n\nThree things the sweep says that the single run could not:\n\n  - A window that is too small LOSES to the exact read (hash 11 > 8 at\n    16 KiB): a window carrying fewer whole frames than the reads it\n    replaces is over-fetch with no return. bfs at 16 KiB only ties.\n  - bfs is never worse than hash on either axis at any window.\n  - Bytes are monotone in the window; reads are monotone the other way.\n    The 128 KiB window is larger than the pack (110 KB) and is the\n    whole-read regime of Q3d: 3 reads, everything fetched.\n\n## Meta\n\nResult: at the largest shape, reads 8 -> 4 (per-object 12, so 3.0x) for\n1.8x the bytes -- and the same reader, over the same blocks in hash order,\n7 reads for 3.6x the bytes. The window is not the win; the ORDER is, and\nthe window is what lets the order be seen. That is the lake lesson stated\nin this harness's own units: Iceberg's sort and Paimon's sorted runs are\nwrite-side locality, and a range reader without it is a bigger fetch.\n\nWhat this iteration did NOT do, deliberately: choose a window size. The\nsweep shows the trade and the trade is a deployment's (bytes versus round\ntrips depend on what the store charges for each). The Worker uses the\ntraversal budget (4 MiB) as its window and Q3h measured that; this harness\nmeasures the shape of the curve.\n\nFindings the loop produced that were not hypotheses:\n\n  - `ayatori.suite` refused to run on the renamed tree: its glob was\n    `_test.clj[cs]` and every test file is now `.cljk`. It refused (exit 2,\n    \"no *_test under test/\") rather than passing on nothing, which is the\n    behaviour its docstring promised and the reason the refusal existed.\n    The glob now admits `.cljk`; the classpath the require then resolves\n    through is the root loader's mirror (scripts/cljk-classpath).\n  - The shared ayatori checkout has no node_modules, so the documented\n    reproduction needs `npm ci` first; `@noble/post-quantum` is the one the\n    envelope tests miss without it.\n\nSeed for iteration 12:\n\n  - H2: partition-aligned packs. bfs order is the single-pack approximation;\n    the real form is one pack per prolly internal node, with P counted per\n    query rather than assumed 1. Needs the write side.\n  - H3: stats in the catalog. The catalog is still an in-memory map in this\n    harness (iteration 03 priced it at +P); giving each entry a key range\n    and counting packs NOT opened is the measurement.\n  - The window's bytes. Exact frame lengths are already in the bounded\n    index; a window could stop at the last frame the traversal will need\n    if the traversal told the reader its frontier. That is a reader/graph\n    seam that does not exist yet.\n"}]
